C1. The specification, with and without the April churn ramp
==============================================================================
  season FE only (no rhythm control)       n  834  ratio 1.560  CI 1.309-1.859  z=4.96  p=0.0000
  + day-of-season bins  <-- THE TEST       n  834  ratio 1.266  CI 0.856-1.872  z=1.18  p=0.2377

  The whole of the naive jump is the shape of April. Every line below asks
  whether the controlled figure comes back somewhere.

==============================================================================
C2. Club fixed effects
==============================================================================
  The claim is about club behaviour, so it must survive a within-club
  comparison. Cells become (club, season, day); a club that simply promotes
  more men cannot contribute.

  club FE, no rhythm control               n  834  ratio 1.560  CI 1.309-1.859  z=4.96  p=0.0000
  club FE + day-of-season bins             n  834  ratio 1.266  CI 0.856-1.872  z=1.18  p=0.2377

  THE TWO ESTIMATES ARE IDENTICAL TO THREE DECIMALS, AND THAT IS ARITHMETIC,
  NOT A PASSED TEST. The line falls on the same date for all thirty clubs in
  a season, so a club dummy is orthogonal to it and cannot move the
  coefficient. Adding club fixed effects to this design answers nothing.
  The within-club question has to be asked a different way: let every club
  have its OWN line coefficient, and test whether they differ.

  club-specific line coefficients: likelihood-ratio X2 = 34.79 on 29 df, p = 0.2112
  No club departs from the common estimate by more than chance, so the
  sample does not support naming one.

  widest club estimates (thin — a median club contributes 28 promotions):
    club 119  promotions  25  before/after the line   1/ 24
    club 143  promotions  17  before/after the line   1/ 16
    club 114  promotions  22  before/after the line   2/ 20

